mdio-rtl-otto: let a board opt a port out of hardware PHY polling

The RTL93xx MDIO block can poll a port's PHY in hardware and feed link state
straight to the MAC.  That is right for a port the switch owns outright, but
wrong for one whose PHY is driven by phylib while phylink owns the switch-side
link through mac_link_up(): the two fight.  The port links, the poller's own
PHY read knocks it back down about a second later, and it stays down - link
shows up, tx increments, and the MAC receives nothing.

On the CR1000A that is lan10g (port 8, AQR113C) and lan1/lan2 (ports 24/20,
RTL8221B).  moca (port 25) is unaffected only because its PHY sits on the SoC
MDIO bus, so this driver already skips it.

Add realtek,no-hw-polling on the port node to leave such a port SerDes-driven,
as rtmd_930x_setup_polling() already initialises every port before deciding
which ones to promote to "PHY driven".

This restores, in upstream's new layout, what the old out-of-tree patch did by
masking ports 8/20/24 out of the switch driver's rtldsa_enable_phy_polling() -
a function that no longer exists, because upstream moved polling setup into
this MDIO driver and configures it at probe.

--- a/mdio-realtek-otto.c
+++ b/mdio-realtek-otto.c
@@ -210,6 +210,7 @@
 struct rtmd_port {
 	int page;
 	bool raw;
+	bool no_hw_poll;
 	u8 smi_addr;
 	u8 smi_bus;
 };
@@ -845,6 +846,16 @@
 
 	/* Define PHY specific polling parameters */
 	for_each_phy_port(ctrl, pn) {
+		/* A port whose PHY is driven by phylib/phylink must stay
+		 * SerDes-driven as set above.  If the hardware poller reads
+		 * it too, the two fight: the port links, the poller's own PHY
+		 * read knocks it back down about a second later, and it stays
+		 * down - the MAC never receives.  phylink owns the switch-side
+		 * link for these ports via mac_link_up().
+		 */
+		if (ctrl->port[pn].no_hw_poll)
+			continue;
+
 		if (rtmd_get_phy_info(ctrl, pn, &phyinfo))
 			continue;
 
@@ -1052,6 +1063,8 @@
 
 		ctrl->port[pn].smi_bus = smi_bus;
 		ctrl->port[pn].smi_addr = smi_addr;
+		ctrl->port[pn].no_hw_poll =
+			fwnode_property_present(fw_port, "realtek,no-hw-polling");
 		__set_bit(pn, ctrl->phy_ports);
 	}
 
